 - `config.yaml` controls episode order. A new episode file is invisible until added to the `episodes:` list. Episode files are unnumbered (`introduction.Rmd`, `raw-data-processing.Rmd`, ...); the numbering in prose and figure names (`ep3_`, `ep5_`) follows the `config.yaml` order.
 - R code lives in real knitr chunks, all `eval=FALSE` (`{r setup, ...}`, `{r set-paths, ...}`, `{r load-data, ...}`, `{r save, ...}`, `{r challenge1, ...}`). Shell code is in plain ```` ```bash ```` display fences. Consequences: (a) `knitr::purl()` extracts the R code in order, but bash blocks must be parsed out of the fences separately; (b) never flip chunks to `eval=TRUE`, the build machine has no data or Seurat stack; (c) figures under `episodes/fig/` are pre-rendered PNGs (`ep<N>_*.png`) and must be regenerated by hand whenever code output changes. `episodes/fig/FIGURE_DESCRIPTIONS.md` is the spec for every figure; `episodes/fig/old_plots/` is gitignored scratch.
 - Episodes 3 to 8 each start with a `set-paths` chunk that builds `work_dir` as `paste0("/scratch/negishi/", Sys.getenv("USER"), "/scrna_workshop/")` (not from `RCAC_SCRATCH`, which episode 2 and setup.md use; same path for a real learner) and calls `setwd(work_dir)` (episode 3 too since patch 01). `saveRDS()` calls are relative to that directory and `readRDS()` calls in the next episode use `paste0(work_dir, ...)`; both depend on `setwd()` having run. The RDS handoff chain is: `pbmc_filtered.rds` (3) -> `pbmc_normalized.rds` (4) -> `pbmc_clustered.rds` (5) -> `pbmc_annotated.rds` (6); then `ifnb_annotated.rds` (7) -> (8). Copies of all five live in the staged `rds/` directory so a learner can start any episode cold, but they land in `scrna_workshop/rds/` and the episodes read `scrna_workshop/<name>.rds`, so the learner has to copy them up first (no episode says so yet; see `updates-patches/05-cold-start-callouts.patch`). Changing an object name or a saved field in one episode requires the matching change in the next episode and in the staged `rds/`.
-- SLURM headers in episode 2 use `--account=workshop --partition=cpu`, `--nodes=1 --ntasks=N`, no `--mem` (RCAC allocates memory by core count; do not add `--mem`). Keep headers identical across the Cell Ranger, STAR index, and STARsolo scripts.
+- SLURM headers in episode 2 use `--account=rcac-rnaseq --partition=cpu` (the same account as the OOD form; `workshop` until patch 09, 2026-10-21), `--nodes=1 --ntasks=N`, no `--mem` (RCAC allocates memory by core count; do not add `--mem`). Keep headers identical across the Cell Ranger, STAR index, and STARsolo scripts. Episode 2 pins `ml cellranger/10.0.0` (patch 08; the Negishi default moved to 10.1.0) to match the whitelist container and the staged matrix; `negishi-run/config.sh` `EXPECTED_MODULES` must list the same module strings (`build_kit.py` checks).
 - Episode markup uses Workbench fenced divs (`questions`, `objectives`, `callout`, `challenge` with nested `solution`, `discussion`, `spoiler`, `keypoints`, `tab`). Every episode needs front matter with `title`, `teaching`, `exercises`.
 - `learners/setup.md` carries the dated day schedule, the instructor list, and the OOD RStudio resource table (app name, R version, account, cores, wall time). Update all of it for every delivery; the OOD app name and R version drift between deliveries.
 
